{
  "image_name": "1fb34be6cc47793638572d7fb00362b5.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several physiological roles of the mechanosensitive ion channel Piezo1 in different tissues and sensory systems. In the upper part, Piezo1 is shown converting mechanical forces such as shear stress or stretch into calcium entry and downstream signaling. In endothelial cells, this signaling appears to regulate focal adhesions, cell migration, cell alignment under flow, and vascular tone through an AKT–eNOS pathway that promotes nitric oxide release and vasodilation. In smooth muscle cells, stretch-triggered calcium signaling leads to transglutaminase activity, extracellular matrix remodeling, and thicker vessel walls. In red blood cells, Piezo1 activation by stretch allows sodium and calcium entry, activates the Gardos channel, and drives potassium efflux and water loss, contributing to cell volume control and dehydration. A developmental example also suggests Piezo1-dependent shear sensing helps organize endothelial cells in the embryo.\n\nThe lower part shows body-wide mechanosensory pathways linking peripheral organs to the nervous system. Signals from skin touch receptors, muscle spindles, and the lung travel through sensory ganglia to the spinal cord or brain. Together, the diagram emphasizes how mechanical stimuli are translated into cellular responses and whole-organism functions including touch, proprioception, breathing, blood flow regulation, and vascular remodeling.",
